== Problem 21 ==
Two distinct lines pass through the center of three concentric circles of radii 3, 2, and 1. The area of the shaded region in the diagram is <imath>\frac{8}{13}</imath> of the area of the unshaded region. What is the radian measure of the acute angle formed by the two lines? (Note: <imath>\pi</imath> radians is <imath>180</imath> degrees.)

<asy> defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair O=origin;
fill(O--Arc(O, 2, 20, 160)--cycle, mediumgray);
fill(O--Arc(O, 1, 20, 160)--cycle, white);
fill(O--Arc(O, 2, 200, 340)--cycle, mediumgray);
fill(O--Arc(O, 1, 200, 340)--cycle, white);
fill(O--Arc(O, 3, 160, 200)--cycle, mediumgray);
fill(O--Arc(O, 2, 160, 200)--cycle, white);
fill(O--Arc(O, 1, 160, 200)--cycle, mediumgray);
fill(O--Arc(O, 3, -20, 20)--cycle, mediumgray);
fill(O--Arc(O, 2, -20, 20)--cycle, white);
fill(O--Arc(O, 1, -20, 20)--cycle, mediumgray);
draw(Circle(origin, 1));draw(Circle(origin, 2));draw(Circle(origin, 3));
draw(5*dir(200)--5*dir(20)^^5*dir(160)--5*dir(-20)); </asy>


<imath> \mathrm{(A) \ } \frac{\pi}{8} \qquad \mathrm{(B) \ } \frac{\pi}{7} \qquad \mathrm{(C) \ } \frac{\pi}{6} \qquad \mathrm{(D) \ } \frac{\pi}{5} \qquad \mathrm{(E) \ } \frac{\pi}{4} </imath>

[[2004 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 21|Solution]]

==Problem 22 ==
[[Square]] <imath>ABCD</imath> has side length <imath>2</imath>. A [[semicircle]] with [[diameter]] <imath>\overline{AB}</imath> is constructed inside the square, and the [[tangent (geometry)|tangent]] to the semicircle from <imath>C</imath> intersects side <imath>\overline{AD}</imath> at <imath>E</imath>. What is the length of <imath>\overline{CE}</imath>?

<asy> defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair A=origin, B=(1,0), C=(1,1), D=(0,1), X=tangent(C, (0.5,0), 0.5, 1), F=C+2*dir(C--X), E=intersectionpoint(C--F, A--D);
draw(C--D--A--B--C--E);
draw(Arc((0.5,0), 0.5, 0, 180));
pair point=(0.5,0.5);
label("$A$", A, dir(point--A));
label("$B$", B, dir(point--B));
label("$C$", C, dir(point--C));
label("$D$", D, dir(point--D));
label("$E$", E, dir(point--E)); </asy>

<imath> \mathrm{(A) \ } \frac{2+\sqrt{5}}{2} \qquad \mathrm{(B) \ } \sqrt{5} \qquad \mathrm{(C) \ } \sqrt{6} \qquad \mathrm{(D) \ } \frac{5}{2} \qquad \mathrm{(E) \ } 5-\sqrt{5} </imath>

